  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The surface area $A$ of a sphere is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing with respect to its radius at the moment when the radius of the sphere is equal to $5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its radius is $5$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We know that the surface area of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(r)=4\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =8\\pi r,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2032}(5)=8\\pi (5)=40\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing when its radius is $5\\,\\text{in}$, is $40\\pi $ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$",
